com/blobfishai/factory-agent-simulation)\\nciteturn18search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; benchmark/factorybench100  | benchmark/factorybench100  |    | ... README.md  | README.md  |    | ... Only version-pinned full-suite model runs are eligible for the public leaderboard. ... The checked-in `benchmark/factorybench100/environment/mcp.json` can be copied directly into Codex, Claude Desktop, or another stdio MCP client. ... Dataset content is CC BY 4.0; see `LICENSE` and `LICENSE-DATA`.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDatasets – Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/datasets?other=factory)\\nciteturn18search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ### Edit Datasets filters ... Orion-The-Lab/wooden_window_factory_01_sample_pack Viewer • Updated Mar 15 • 10 • 73 daneel-data/factory-manipulation-sample-v1 Updated May 21 • 13 TrainThemAI/Industrial-Workplace-Egocentric-FHD-Samples Viewer • Updated May 28 • 42 • 172 SeongryongJung/factory-agent-rollouts Preview • Updated Jun 2 • 86 daneel-data/factory-manipulation-sample-v2 Updated Jun 18 • 9 VerboseTechLabs/manufacturing-unit-egocentric-sample Updated 25 days ago • 129\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGitHub - factorynet0/FactoryNet · GitHub (https://github.com/factorynet0/FactoryNet)\\nciteturn18search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # FactoryNet — experiment code ...   * Include heavy 130-signal MLP: `python scripts/run_paper_numbers.py --full` ... Manuscript (PDF/TeX) is not in this repository.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation Models | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2605.09081v2)\\nciteturn18search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week; @misc{othman2026factorynetlargescaledatasetindustrial, title={FactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation Models}, author={Karim Othman and Jonas Petersen and Matei Ignuta-Ciuncanu and Camilla Mazzoleni and Federico Martelli and Alessandro Lombardi and Riccardo Maggioni and Philipp Petersen}, year={2026}, eprint={2605.09081}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.LG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09081}, }\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation Models (https://alex-wiki.langotime.ai/sources/factorynet-2026)\\nciteturn18search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday;   * Preprint: https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09081v4 ...   * Official project page: https://www.forgis.com/papers/factorynet.html\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nfactory-agent-simulation/benchmark/factorybench100/verifiers/factorybench-056.json at main · blobfishai/factory-agent-simulation · GitHub (https://github.com/blobfishai/factory-agent-simulation/blob/main/benchmark/factorybench100/verifiers/factorybench-056.json)\\nciteturn18search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # factorybench-056.json ... \\\"description\\\": \\\"Determine the operative R1 requirement for NS-000056, reconcile the current source material, and distinguish current evidence from superseded or merely contextual records.\\\",\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ndblp: FactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation Models. (https://dblp1.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2605-09081.html)\\nciteturn18search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # \\\"FactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation ...\\\" ... DOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2605.09081\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGitHub - xuanliugit/FGBench: [NeurIPS'25] FGBench: A Dataset and Benchmark for Molecular Property Reasoning at Functional Group-Level in Large Language Models · GitHub (https://github.com/xuanliugit/FGBench)\\nciteturn18search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; README.md  | README.md  |    | ... # FGBench: A Dataset and Benchmark for Molecular Property Reasoning at Functional Group-Level in Large Language Models\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation Models (https://openreview.net/pdf/5a1bffc72e1fa4297b1946cd709e448d0062f516.pdf)\\nciteturn18search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; FactoryNet: A Large-Scale Dataset toward Industrial Time-Series Foundation Models ... To reduce dependence on real-only data, FactoryNet in- ... For voraus-AD anomaly detection, we follow the official\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDataset Background: (https://huggingface.co/datasets/morpheushoc/something-something-v2/resolve/main/20bn-something-something_download_instructions_-_091622.pdf?download=true)\\nciteturn18search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Dataset Background:The dataset was created with the help of more than 5,400 unique crowd actors. ... In order to extract the TGZ archive, the 20 files must be downloaded and concatenated in the right order.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n[14]: `dataset.push_to_hub(hf_repo_name)` (https://huggingface.co/julep-ai/dfe-base-en/resolve/refs%2Fpr%2F1/data_prep.pdf?download=true)\\nciteturn18search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Deleting unused files from dataset repository: `0%| | 0/1 [00:00\u003c?, ? ... Deleting unused files from dataset repository: `0%| | 0/1 [00:00\u003c?, ?\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe Stack: 3 TB of permissively licensed source code (https://huggingface.co/api/resolve-cache/datasets/bigcode/admin/01c3e2619369a3fbe5225419f84f2d124be4b26b/The_Stack.pdf?%2Fdatasets%2Fbigcode%2Fadmin%2Fresolve%2Fmain%2FThe_Stack.pdf=\u0026etag=%2208b9e9f7eb9992ffd9bfa98686e352718f95209a%22)\\nciteturn18search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; GitHub data is also publicly available on the HuggingFace hub4 as the GitHub-Code dataset ... provide the exact list of excluded binary file extensions in Appendix B. ... 4https://huggingface.co/datasets/codeparrot/github-code\\n\"","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa6454c01e887d2947cab6c0ed481ed","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_R0zBdX77OPCHt7MZVUYDu5VJ","id":"ctc_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa6454cb7d887d2a9da5194c87669e6","input":"const r = await tools.web__run({open:[\n {ref_id:\"turn18search0\"},\n {ref_id:\"turn18search1\"},\n {ref_id:\"turn18search6\"},\n {ref_id:\"https://www.forgis.com/papers/factorynet.html\"}\n],response_length:\"long\"}); text(JSON.stringify(r));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789281610.248172,"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_R0zBdX77OPCHt7MZVUYDu5VJ","id":"ctco_01a0997e-ba5e-79e0-8cd8-232e94ff5c09","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789281614.430095,"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.0 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\"FactoryBench/README.md at main · Forgis-Labs/FactoryBench · GitHub (https://github.com/Forgis-Labs/FactoryBench/blob/main/README.md)\\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn18search0\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 434\\nL58:     * EXPLORE BY TOPIC\\nL59:       * cite34†AI L60:       * cite35†Software Development L71:     * SUPPORT \u0026 SERVICES\\nL72:       * cite14†Documentation†docs.github.com L73:       * cite44†Customer support†support.github.com L74:       * cite45†Community forum L75:       * cite46†Trust center L76:       * cite47†Partners L77: \\nL78: cite48†View all resources L79: \\nL80:   * Open Source\\nL81: \\nL82:     * COMMUNITY\\nL83:       * cite49†GitHub SponsorsFund open source developers L84:     * PROGRAMS\\nL85:       * cite50†Security Lab†securitylab.github.com L86:       * cite51†Maintainer Community†maintainers.github.com L87:       * cite52†GitHub Stars†stars.github.com L88:       * cite53†Archive Program†archiveprogram.github.com L89: \\nL90:     * REPOSITORIES\\nL91:       * cite54†Topics L92:       * cite55†Trending L93:       * cite56†Collections L94: \\nL95:   * Enterprise\\nL96: \\nL97:     * ENTERPRISE SOLUTIONS\\nL98:       * cite19†Enterprise platformAI-powered developer platform L99:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\\nL100:       * cite10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features L101:       * cite57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features L102:       * cite58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support L103: \\nL104:   * cite59†Pricing L105: \\nL106: Search`/`\\nL107: \\nL108: cite1†Sign in L109: \\nL110: cite60†Sign up L111: \\nL112: Appearance settings\\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\\nL114: ### Uh oh!\\nL115: \\nL116: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\\nL117: \\nL118: cite61†Forgis-Labs / cite62†FactoryBench Public\\nL119: \\nL120:   * cite63†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\\nL121:   * cite63†Fork 0 L122:   * cite63†Star L123: \\nL124:   * cite62†Code L125:   * cite64†Issues 0 L126:   * cite65†Pull requests 0 L127:   * cite66†Actions L128:   * cite67†Projects L129:   * cite68†Security and quality 0 L130:   * cite69†Insights L131: \\nL132: Additional navigation options\\nL133: \\nL134: ## Files Expand file tree\\nL135: \\nL136: main\\nL137: \\nL138: ## Breadcrumbs\\nL139: \\nL140:   1. cite70†FactoryBench L141: \\nL142: /\\nL143: # README.md\\nL144: \\nL145: Copy path\\nL146: \\nL147: [Input: Go to file]\\nL148: \\nL149: Blame More file actions\\nL150: \\nL151: Blame More file actions\\nL152: \\nL153: ## Latest commit\\nL154: \\nL155: \\nL156: \\nL157: ## History\\nL158: \\nL159: History\\nL160: \\nL161: main\\nL162: \\nL163: ## Breadcrumbs\\nL164: \\nL165:   1. cite70†FactoryBench L166: \\nL167: /\\nL168: \\nL169: # README.md\\nL170: \\nL171: Copy path\\nL172: \\nL173: Top\\nL174: \\nL175: ## File metadata and controls\\nL176: \\nL177:   * Preview\\nL178: \\nL179:   * Code\\nL180: \\nL181:   * Blame\\nL182: \\nL183: 367 lines (285 loc) · 14.7 KB\\nL184: \\nL185: cite71†Raw L186: \\nL187: Copy raw file\\nL188: \\nL189: Download raw file\\nL190: \\nL191: Outline\\nL192: \\nL193: Edit and raw actions\\nL194: # factorybench\\nL195: \\nL196: Python library and CLI for evaluating language models on the FactoryBench benchmark of industrial-machine reasoning across four causal levels (state / intervention / counterfactual / decision). Dataset: cite72†`FactoryBench/FactoryBench`†huggingface.co on the Hugging Face Hub.\\nL197: What's included: dataset loader, prompt renderer, deterministic scorer for Levels 1-3, opt-in LLM-as-judge ensemble for Level 4 (with cache + Fleiss' kappa), built-in OpenAI / Anthropic / DeepSeek adapters, cost preview, model comparison, and a CLI covering the full evaluate / export / score / compare workflow. See `CHANGELOG.md` for what's landed.\\nL198: ## Install\\nL199: \\nL200:     pip install -e .                       # base install (mock model only)\\nL201:     pip install -e \\\".[openai]\\\"             # adds OpenAI / DeepSeek (gpt-*, deepseek-*)\\nL202:     pip install -e \\\".[anthropic]\\\"          # adds Anthropic (claude-*)\\nL203:     pip install -e \\\".[all]\\\"                # everything\\nL204:     pip install -e \\\".[dev]\\\"                # pytest for running the test suite\\nL205: The base install includes `tiktoken` for tiktoken-counted cost previews; if it ever fails to import (air-gapped environments, lazy BPE-download failure on first use), the library falls back to a `len(text) / 4` heuristic and surfaces that in the preview output.\\nL206: \\nL207: Python 3.10+.\\nL208: ### Provider credentials\\nL209: \\nL210: Set whichever provider keys you need before running:\\nL211: \\nL212:     # bash / zsh\\nL213:     export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...\\nL214:     export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...\\nL215:     export DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=...\\nL216: \\nL217:     # PowerShell\\nL218:     $env:OPENAI_API_KEY    = 'sk-...'\\nL219:     $env:ANTHROPIC_API_KEY = 'sk-ant-...'\\nL220:     $env:DEEPSEEK_API_KEY  = '...'\\nL221: `factorybench info` shows which providers are configured and prints the exact `export` / `$env:` line for any that are missing. The library reads keys from the environment only -- it does not auto-load `.env` files. If you keep keys in a `.env`, source it yourself (`source .env` in bash, or `python-dotenv` in your own scripts) before invoking `factorybench` -- this matches the behavior of the underlying OpenAI / Anthropic SDKs and avoids surprising configuration.\\nL222: ## Quick start (Python)\\nL223: \\nL224:     from factorybench import register_model, evaluate\\nL225: \\nL226:     @register_model(\\\"dummy\\\")\\nL227:     class Dummy:\\nL228:         def predict(self, prompt: str) -\u003e str:\\nL229:             return \\\"0\\\"\\nL230: \\nL231:     result = evaluate(model=\\\"dummy\\\", level=\\\"L1\\\", max_items=20)\\nL232:     print(result.score)\\nL233:     print(result.by_level())\\nL234:     print(result.by_answer_format())\\nL235:     df = result.to_dataframe()\\nL236: \\nL237: `evaluate(...)` accepts:\\nL238:   * `model`: a registered model name, a built-in provider spec (`\\\"mock\\\"`, `\\\"gpt-5.1\\\"`, `\\\"claude-sonnet-4.6\\\"`, `\\\"deepseek-chat\\\"`, ...), or any object with `predict(prompt) -\u003e str` (optionally `predict_batch(prompts) -\u003e list[str]`).\\nL239:   * `level`: `\\\"L1\\\" | \\\"L2\\\" | \\\"L3\\\" | \\\"all\\\"` (L4 is rejected — needs a judge ensemble).\\nL240:   * `split`: `\\\"test\\\"` (default), `\\\"validation\\\"`, `\\\"train\\\"`, or `\\\"mini\\\"` (50 items per level).\\nL241:   * `max_items`: cap per level (useful for smoke tests).\\nL242:   * `progress`: show a tqdm bar (default `True`).\\nL243: ## CLI\\nL244: \\nL245:     factorybench info                                            # version, dataset revision, cache, providers, prices\\nL246:     factorybench info --json                                     # machine-readable (paste into bug reports)\\nL247:     factorybench cache stats                                     # judge cache size + age\\nL248:     factorybench cache clear -y                                  # wipe the judge cache\\nL249:     factorybench prices list                                     # bundled price table + version stamp\\nL250:     factorybench prices source                                   # show where prices are loaded from\\nL251:     factorybench list models                                     # registered + built-in\\nL252:     factorybench list levels                                     # item counts per level\\nL253:     factorybench list templates --level L2                       # templates within a level\\nL254:     # online evaluation against a provider\\nL255:     factorybench evaluate --model claude-sonnet-4.6 --split mini\\nL256:     factorybench evaluate --model gpt-5.1 --level L2 --max-items 50\\nL257:     factorybench evaluate --model mock --level all --split mini  # no API needed\\nL258: \\nL259:     # user-registered model from a script file\\nL260:     factorybench evaluate --model my-model --import ./my_models.py --split mini\\nL261: \\nL262:     # offline workflow (run inference yourself)\\nL263:     factorybench export --level L2 --output prompts.jsonl\\nL264:     # ... run your model over prompts.jsonl, writing {item_id, prediction} JSONL ...\\nL265:     factorybench score --predictions predictions.jsonl --level L2 --output scores.json\\nL266: Built-in provider specs map to:\\nL267: \\nL268: Pattern  | Adapter  | Required env var\\nL269: --- | --- | ---\\nL270: `mock`  | Canned-response (no API)  | --\\nL271: `gpt-*`  | OpenAI chat completions  | `OPENAI_API_KEY`\\nL272: `claude-*`  | Anthropic Messages API  | `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`\\nL273: `deepseek-*`  | DeepSeek (OpenAI-compatible)  | `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`\\nL274: ## Scoring\\nL275: \\nL276: Each item is scored 0--1, then aggregated with a chance-corrected accuracy:\\nL277: \\nL278:     `score = (mean_accuracy - mean_chance) / (1 - mean_chance)\\nL279:     `\\nL280: \\nL281: Chance rates per answer format:\\nL282: \\nL283: Format  | Chance\\nL284: --- | ---\\nL285: `single_letter_mcq`  | `1 / #options`\\nL286: `four_letter_tf`  | `0.5` (per-element TF)\\nL287: `four_letter_ranking`  | `1/24` (24 permutations of A,B,C,D)\\nL288: `scalar_range`  | `0`\\nL289: `scalar_margin`  | `0`\\nL290: `tensor_margin`  | `0`\\nL291: `scalar_exact`  | `0`\\nL309: Judges run in parallel by default (one thread per judge in the panel, capped at 8). Override with `--judge-concurrency N` -- set `1` to force sequential, or raise it for very large ensembles. Each judge is a different provider, so they don't share rate limits and parallelism is a free ~3x speedup on the paper-default ensemble.\\nL310: \\nL311: By default, `--level all` skips L4 unless `--judges` is also given.\\nL333: Same three options are available on the Python API:\\nL334: \\nL335:     result = fb.evaluate(\\nL336:         model=\\\"gpt-5.1\\\",\\nL337:         level=\\\"L4\\\",\\nL338:         judges=\\\"paper-default\\\",\\nL339:         concurrency=8,\\nL340:         resume_from=\\\"results/my-run.json\\\",\\nL341:         judge_cache_only=False,\\nL342:     )\\nL393: After running several models on the same split, drop a model x level table into your paper:\\nL394: \\nL395:     from factorybench import evaluate, compare\\nL396: \\nL397:     results = {\\nL398:         \\\"gpt-5.1\\\":            evaluate(model=\\\"gpt-5.1\\\",           split=\\\"mini\\\"),\\nL399:         \\\"claude-sonnet-4.6\\\":  evaluate(model=\\\"claude-sonnet-4.6\\\", split=\\\"mini\\\"),\\nL400:         \\\"my-model\\\":           evaluate(model=\\\"my-model\\\",          split=\\\"mini\\\"),\\nL401:     }\\nL402:     comp = compare(results)\\nL403:     print(comp.to_markdown())                # paste into a draft\\nL416:   * Prompt caching on the Anthropic adapter -- each FactoryBench item has a unique time-series body, and the shared prefix per level (description + acronym mapping) is well below the 2K / 4K minimum cacheable prefix on Sonnet / Opus. Adding `cache_control` here would write entries that are never read.\\nL421: If you use FactoryBench in academic work, please cite the paper:\\nL422: \\nL423:     @article{factorybench2026,\\nL424:       title   = {FactoryBench: Evaluating Industrial Machine Understanding},\\nL425:       author  = {Merzouki, Yanis and Izquierdo, Coral and Ignuta-Ciuncanu, Matei and G{\\\\'o}mez-Bracamonte, Marcos and Maggioni, Riccardo and Lombardi, Alessandro and Mazzoleni, Camilla and Martelli, Federico and G{\\\\\\\"u}nther, Bal{\\\\'a}zs and Petersen, Jonas and Petersen, Philipp},\\nL426:       journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.07675},\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nREADME.md · FactoryBench/FactoryBench at main (https://huggingface.co/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/blob/main/README.md)\\nciteturn19view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn18search1\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 299\\nL141:       - config_name: lite_level_2\\nL142:         data_files:\\nL143:           - split: train\\nL144:             path: factorybench_lite/level_2.jsonl\\nL145:       - config_name: lite_level_3\\nL146:         data_files:\\nL147:           - split: train\\nL148:             path: factorybench_lite/level_3.jsonl\\nL149:       - config_name: lite_level_4\\nL150:         data_files:\\nL151:           - split: train\\nL152:             path: factorybench_lite/level_4.jsonl\\nL153: # FactoryBench\\nL154: \\nL155: FactoryBench is a benchmark for evaluating machine-behavior reasoning in time-series models and LLMs over industrial robotic telemetry. Question-answer pairs are organised along the four levels of Pearl's causal hierarchy:\\nL156: Level  | Capability  | Example\\nL157: --- | --- | ---\\nL158: L1 — State  | Identify the operational state from raw signals  | \\\"Which fault, if any, is occurring in this episode?\\\"\\nL159: L2 — Intervention  | Predict the effect of an intervention  | \\\"How would the joint torques change if the payload were doubled?\\\"\\nL160: L3 — Counterfactual  | Reason about alternative histories  | \\\"Would the collision still have occurred if the speed had been 50% lower?\\\"\\nL161: L4 — Decision  | Engineering decision-making (troubleshooting + optimisation)  | \\\"Given this anomaly, what is the most likely root cause and remediation?\\\"\\nL162: The benchmark is grounded in FactoryWave, a dense multivariate telemetry dataset collected from a UR3 collaborative robot (125 Hz) and a KUKA KR10 industrial arm (83 Hz), supplemented with the AURSAD and voraus-AD open-source datasets.\\nL163: ## Dataset summary\\nL164:   * 69,691 Q\u0026A pairs across four causal levels, released as a single undivided pool (the public release is not split into train/validation/test).\\nL165:   * FactoryBench-Lite: a balanced 3,000-item subset for cheap evaluation, even across templates and within each template on the dimension that determines its answer.\\nL166:   * 5 answer formats: single-select MCQ, multi-select MCQ, ranking, tensor/numerical, free-form (judged by an LLM-as-judge voting protocol).\\nL167:   * Telemetry from real industrial robots with systematic fault injection (27 atomic mechanisms across pick-and-place, screwing, and peg-in-hole tasks).\\nL168: ## Repository layout\\nL169: \\nL170:     `FactoryBench/\\nL171:     ├── factorybench_qa/                 # Question-answer pairs (full pool)\\nL172:     │   ├── level_1.jsonl\\nL173:     │   ├── level_2.jsonl\\nL174:     │   ├── level_3.jsonl\\nL175:     │   └── level_4.jsonl\\nL176:     ├── factorybench_lite/               # Balanced 3,000-item evaluation subset\\nL177:     │   ├── level_1.jsonl\\nL178:     │   ├── level_2.jsonl\\nL179:     │   ├── level_3.jsonl\\nL180:     │   └── level_4.jsonl\\nL181:     ├── knowledge_graph/                 # Combined knowledge graph\\nL182:     │   ├── knowledge_graph.json         # Machines, grippers, tasks, events, faults, anomalies, error→protocol map, relevance specs\\nL183:     │   └── SCHEMA.md                    # Field-level schema documentation\\nL184:     └── factorywave/                     # Underlying telemetry \u0026 metadata\\nL185:         ├── episodes.parquet             # Episode-level metadata (9,728 episodes)\\nL186:         ├── flow.parquet                 # Task flow definitions\\nL187:         ├── kuka_signals.parquet         # KUKA KR10 signals (~83 Hz, 1,428 episodes)\\nL188:         ├── ur_signals.parquet           # UR3 signals (~125 Hz, 3,076 episodes)\\nL189:         ├── ur_signals_10hz.parquet      # UR3 signals (10 Hz, 3,984 episodes — disjoint from ur_signals)\\nL190:         └── ur_screwdriver_signals.parquet  # UR3 screwdriver subset (~125 Hz, 1,240 episodes)\\nL191:     `\\nL192: \u003e Note on UR coverage. `ur_signals.parquet` (~125 Hz) and `ur_signals_10hz.parquet` (10 Hz) cover disjoint UR3 episode subsets — no episode appears in both files. Together they span 7,060 distinct UR3 episodes; `ur_screwdriver_signals.parquet` adds another 1,240 screwdriver-task episodes. Each row in `episodes.parquet` corresponds to exactly one signal table.\\nL193: ## Knowledge graph\\nL194: `knowledge_graph/knowledge_graph.json` is the structured \\\"world model\\\" that grounds FactoryBench Q\u0026A items: machine and gripper capability tables, the task/event vocabulary, the fault root-cause catalogue, the observable-anomaly catalogue, the error→protocol mapping used to derive ground-truth answers for L4 troubleshooting items, an anomaly severity ranking, and the relevance specs that drive fault-aware sub-series sampling. See cite57†`knowledge_graph/SCHEMA.md` for field-level documentation.\\nL195:     `import json, urllib.request\\nL196:     url = \\\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/resolve/main/knowledge_graph/knowledge_graph.json\\\"\\nL197:     kg = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(url).read())\\nL198: \\nL199:     # Machine spec lookup\\nL200:     machines_by_id = {m[\\\"machine_id\\\"]: m for m in kg[\\\"machines\\\"]}\\nL201: \\nL202:     # Error → operator protocol (the ground truth for L4 troubleshooting answers)\\nL203:     protocol_for = {e[\\\"root_cause\\\"]: e[\\\"ur3_protocol\\\"] for e in kg[\\\"root_cause_error_mapping\\\"]}\\nL204:     print(protocol_for[\\\"collision_rigid_object\\\"])\\nL205:     `\\nL206: ## Q\u0026A pair counts\\nL207: \\nL208: Level  | Full pool  | Lite\\nL209: --- | --- | ---\\nL210: L1  | 15,321  | 572\\nL211: L2  | 40,226  | 1,430\\nL212: L3  | 2,939  | 712\\nL213: L4  | 11,205  | 286\\nL214: Total  | 69,691  | 3,000\\nL215: \\nL216: The public release is a single undivided pool per level. The `train` split name in the config block is the Hugging Face default for a single-file config, not a semantic split. A private 15% slice is held back for contamination checks and is not counted here.\\nL217: ## Q\u0026A fields\\nL218: \\nL219: Each line in `factorybench_qa/level_*.jsonl` (and `factorybench_lite/level_*.jsonl`) is a single Q\u0026A item:\\nL220: Field  | Description\\nL221: --- | ---\\nL222: `id`  | Unique item identifier\\nL223: `level`  | Causal level (1–4)\\nL224: `template_id`  | Question template the item was generated from\\nL225: `template_type`  | Answer format (`single_choice`, `multi_choice`, `ranking`, `tensor`, `free_form`)\\nL226: `hides`  | Channels/fields hidden from the model in this item\\nL227: `question`  | Natural-language question\\nL228: `options`  | Answer options (for MCQ/ranking templates)\\nL229: `answer`  | Ground-truth answer\\nL230: `root_cause`  | Underlying fault/cause (Level 4 only)\\nL231: `acceptance_bounds`  | Tolerance for numerical answers\\nL232: `provenance`  | Source episode(s) and channels used to derive the item\\nL233: `context`  | Time-series and metadata context exposed to the model\\nL234: ## Loading the data\\nL235: \\nL236:     `from datasets import load_dataset\\nL237: \\nL238:     # Load one level of the full pool\\nL239:     ds = load_dataset(\\\"FactoryBench/FactoryBench\\\", \\\"level_1\\\", split=\\\"train\\\")\\nL240: \\nL241:     # Load one level of the balanced Lite subset\\nL242:     lite = load_dataset(\\\"FactoryBench/FactoryBench\\\", \\\"lite_level_1\\\", split=\\\"train\\\")\\nL243: \\nL244:     # Or address a file directly\\nL245:     ds = load_dataset(\\nL246:         \\\"FactoryBench/FactoryBench\\\",\\nL247:         data_files=\\\"factorybench_qa/level_1.jsonl\\\",\\nL248:         split=\\\"train\\\",\\nL249:     )\\nL250:     # Load underlying telemetry\\nL251:     import pandas as pd\\nL252:     root = \\\"hf://datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/factorywave\\\"\\nL253:     episodes = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/episodes.parquet\\\")\\nL254:     ur_125 = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/ur_signals.parquet\\\")\\nL255:     ur_10  = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/ur_signals_10hz.parquet\\\")  # different episodes\\nL256:     kuka   = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/kuka_signals.parquet\\\")\\nL257:     screw  = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/ur_screwdriver_signals.parquet\\\")\\nL258:     # Load the combined knowledge graph (machines, faults, error→protocol, ...)\\nL259:     import json, urllib.request\\nL260:     kg = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(\\nL261:         \\\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/resolve/main/knowledge_graph/knowledge_graph.json\\\"\\nL262:     ).read())\\nL263:     `\\nL264: ## Citation\\nL265: If you use FactoryBench, please cite the dataset and the two upstream open-source datasets it incorporates (AURSAD and voraus-AD):\\nL266: \\nL267:     `@misc{anonymous2026factorybench,\\nL268:       title  = {FactoryBench: Evaluating Industrial Machine Understanding},\\nL269:       author = {Anonymous},\\nL270:       year   = {2026},\\nL271:       note   = {Submission under double-blind review}\\nL272:     }\\nL273: \\nL274:     @article{leporowski2022aursad,\\nL275:       title   = {{AURSAD}: Universal Robot Screwdriving Anomaly Detection Dataset},\\nL276:       author  = {Leporowski, B{\\\\l}a{\\\\.z}ej and Tola, Daniella and Hansen, Christian and Iosifidis, Alexandros},\\nL277:       journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.03211},\\nL278:       year    = {2022}\\nL279:     }\\nL280: \\nL281:     @misc{brockmann2024vorausad,\\nL282:       title         = {voraus-{AD}: A New Dataset for Anomaly Detection in Robot Applications},\\nL283:       author        = {Brockmann, Jan Thie{\\\\ss} and Rudolph, Marco and Rosenhahn, Bodo and Wandt, Bastian},\\nL284:       year          = {2024},\\nL285:       eprint        = {2311.04153},\\nL286:       archivePrefix = {arXiv},\\nL287:       primaryClass  = {cs.RO}\\nL288:     }\\nL289:     `\\nL290: ## Intended use \u0026 limitations\\nL291: \\nL292: Intended use. Benchmark evaluation of LLMs and time-series models on structured industrial Q\u0026A reasoning tasks (state, intervention, counterfactual, decision-making).\\nL293: Limitations. Domain-specific to factory and industrial robotic scenarios; may not generalise to open-domain Q\u0026A. Faults are atomic and drawn from a closed catalogue of 27 physically injected mechanisms — different from compound or gradual real-world faults. The dataset shows a size imbalance between Levels 2 and 3.\\nL294: \\nL295: Out-of-scope. Not intended for deployment in safety-critical, medical, legal, or financial decision systems without further validation by domain experts.\\nL296: ## License\\nL297: \\nL298: Released under the cite58†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)†creativecommons.org license. You may share and adapt the dataset for any purpose, including commercial, with appropriate attribution to the FactoryBench authors. The accompanying generator source code, evaluation scripts, LLM-as-judge prompts, and tooling (linked from the paper) are released separately under the MIT License.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGitHub - factorynet0/FactoryNet · GitHub (https://github.com/factorynet0/FactoryNet)\\nciteturn19view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn18search6\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 240\\nL84:     * PROGRAMS\\nL85:       * cite50†Security Lab†securitylab.github.com L86:       * cite51†Maintainer Community†maintainers.github.com L87:       * cite52†GitHub Stars†stars.github.com L88:       * cite53†Archive Program†archiveprogram.github.com L89: \\nL90:     * REPOSITORIES\\nL91:       * cite54†Topics L92:       * cite55†Trending L93:       * cite56†Collections L94: \\nL95:   * Enterprise\\nL96: \\nL97:     * ENTERPRISE SOLUTIONS\\nL98:       * cite19†Enterprise platformAI-powered developer platform L99:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\\nL100:       * cite10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features L101:       * cite57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features L102:       * cite58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support L103: \\nL104:   * cite59†Pricing L105: \\nL106: Search`/`\\nL107: \\nL108: cite1†Sign in L109: \\nL110: cite60†Sign up L111: \\nL112: Appearance settings\\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\\nL114: \\nL115: cite61†factorynet0 / cite62†FactoryNet Public\\nL116: \\nL117:   * cite63†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\\nL118:   * cite63†Fork 0 L119:   * cite63†Star L120:   * cite62†Code L121:   * cite64†Issues 0 L122:   * cite65†Pull requests 0 L123:   * cite66†Actions L124:   * cite67†Projects L125:   * cite68†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite69†Insights L127: \\nL128: Additional navigation options\\nL129: \\nL130: main\\nL131: \\nL132: cite70†Branches cite71†Tags L133: \\nL134: [Input: Go to file]\\nL135: \\nL136: Go to file\\nL137: \\nL138: Code\\nL139: \\nL140: Open more actions menu\\nL141: ## Latest commit\\nL142: \\nL143: \\nL144: \\nL145: ## History\\nL146: \\nL147: 1 Commit\\nL148: \\nL149: ## Folders and files\\nL150: \\nL151: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\\nL152: --- | --- | --- | ---\\nL153: cite72†analysis/voraus-ad | cite72†analysis/voraus-ad |    |\\nL154: cite73†config | cite73†config |    |\\nL155: cite74†data | cite74†data |    |\\nL156: cite75†docs | cite75†docs |    |\\nL157: cite76†experiments | cite76†experiments |    |\\nL158: cite77†scripts | cite77†scripts |    |\\nL159: cite78†README.md | cite78†README.md |    |\\nL160: cite79†requirements.txt | cite79†requirements.txt |    |\\nL161: ## Repository files navigation\\nL162: \\nL163:   *   * cite80†README L164: \\nL165: More items\\nL166: # FactoryNet — experiment code\\nL167: \\nL168:     `├── config/                 # env.example\\nL169:     ├── docs/                   # NEEDS, DATA, EXPERIMENTS\\nL170:     ├── experiments/\\nL171:     │   ├── forecasting/        # TCN-Transformer, rollouts, AURSAD transfer\\nL172:     │   └── anomaly/            # voraus S-E-F-C MLP (+ optional notebook)\\nL173:     ├── scripts/\\nL174:     │   ├── run_paper_numbers.py   # ← one command for all paper metrics\\nL175:     │   ├── download_data.py\\nL176:     │   ├── run_forecasting.py\\nL177:     │   └── run_anomaly.py\\nL178:     ├── data/                   # (filled by download_data.py)\\nL179:     ├── requirements.txt\\nL180:     └── README.md\\nL181:     `\\nL182: ## Paper numbers (one flow)\\nL183: \\nL184: From the repository root, after `pip install -r requirements.txt`:\\nL185: \\nL186:     # First time: fetch data, then train/eval everything (long run)\\nL187:     python scripts/run_paper_numbers.py --download\\nL188: \\nL189:     # Later: data already in ./data/\\nL190:     python scripts/run_paper_numbers.py\\nL191: \\nL192: Optional:\\nL193:   * Save output: `python scripts/run_paper_numbers.py 2\u003e\u00261 | tee paper_run.log`\\nL194:   * Include heavy 130-signal MLP: `python scripts/run_paper_numbers.py --full`\\nL195:   * Hub skip / raw voraus only: set `REPRO_HF_SKIP=1` before `--download`, or use `download_data.py --no-hf`\\nL196: \\nL197: What to read in the terminal: forecasting table (MSE/MAE, survival), transfer blocks (6.2 steps, effort MAE, MC-MAE), then anomaly AUROC tables. Map: cite81†docs/EXPERIMENTS.md . Author reference log: cite82†experiments/forecasting/results.txt .\\nL198: ## Piecemeal\\nL199: \\nL200:   * Data only: `python scripts/download_data.py`\\nL201:   * Forecasting only: `python scripts/run_forecasting.py`\\nL202:   * Anomaly only: `python scripts/run_anomaly.py`\\nL203: \\nL204: Prerequisites / paths: cite83†docs/NEEDS.md , cite84†docs/DATA.md , cite85†config/env.example L205: \\nL206: Manuscript (PDF/TeX) is not in this repository.\\nL207: \\nL208: ## About\\nL209: \\nL210: No description, website, or topics provided.\\nL211: \\nL212: ### Resources\\nL213: \\nL214: cite86†Readme L215: \\nL216: cite87†Activity L217: \\nL218: ### Stars\\nL219: \\nL220: 2 stars\\nL221: \\nL222: ### Watchers\\nL223: \\nL224: 0 watching\\nL225: \\nL226: ### Forks\\nL227: \\nL228: cite88†0 forks L229: \\nL230: cite89†Report repository L231: ## Releases\\nL232: \\nL233: ## Packages\\nL234: \\nL235: ## Contributors\\nL236: \\nL237: ## Languages\\nL238: \\nL239: You can’t perform that action at this time.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFactoryNet · Forgis Labs (https://www.forgis.com/papers/factorynet.html)\\nciteturn19view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://www.forgis.com/papers/factorynet.html\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 108\\n\"","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa645520e1c87d299ec9ec6dcd2e628","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"summary":[{"text":"**Evaluating training methods**\n\nOkay, the user wants a broad and calculated approach. I need to think about running the base OpenTSLM on FactoryBench mini through official benchmarks using textual prompts. However, that might not showcase the temporal numeric model effectively. \n\nIt could be better to fine-tune a generative LLM directly on FactoryBench contexts rather than OpenTSLM. There’s a lot to consider about using FactoryNet and the training risks involved, especially since FactoryBench has hundreds of episodes for more effective supervised multi-task learning. I need to ensure any integration is ready within time constraints.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa64559b08887d2af62e6c0d14e3e8e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"summary":[{"text":"**Evaluating training methods**\n\nOkay, the user wants a broad and calculated approach. I need to think about running the base OpenTSLM on FactoryBench mini through official benchmarks using textual prompts. However, that might not showcase the temporal numeric model effectively. \n\nIt could be better to fine-tune a generative LLM directly on FactoryBench contexts rather than OpenTSLM. There’s a lot to consider about using FactoryNet and the training risks involved, especially since FactoryBench has hundreds of episodes for more effective supervised multi-task learning. I need to ensure any integration is ready within time constraints.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_zYALheM9uay70nIg1NjTUUog","id":"ctc_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa6455aca4487d2b932a139dc71c3ef","input":"const r = await Promise.allSettled([\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"curl -sSIL https://huggingface.co/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/resolve/main/factorybench_lite/level_1.jsonl\",workdir:\"/Users/sametdegirmenci/Downloads/ysamet\",yield_time_ms:30000,max_output_tokens:5000,sandbox_permissions:\"require_escalated\",justification:\"May I inspect the official FactoryBench Lite artifact size and availability before preparing a training job?\",\"prefix_rule\":[\"curl\",\"-sSIL\"]}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"curl -sS https://huggingface.co/api/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/tree/main/factorybench_lite?recursive=false\u0026expand=false\",workdir:\"/Users/sametdegirmenci/Downloads/ysamet\",yield_time_ms:30000,max_output_tokens:8000,sandbox_permissions:\"require_escalated\",justification:\"May I inspect the official FactoryBench file manifest for exact training artifacts?\",\"prefix_rule\":[\"curl\",\"-sS\"]}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"curl -sS https://huggingface.co/api/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/tree/main/factorywave?recursive=false\u0026expand=false\",workdir:\"/Users/sametdegirmenci/Downloads/ysamet\",yield_time_ms:30000,max_output_tokens:8000,sandbox_permissions:\"require_escalated\",justification:\"May I inspect the official FactoryBench telemetry file manifest and sizes?\",\"prefix_rule\":[\"curl\",\"-sS\"]})\n]);\nfor (const x of r) text(x.status === \"fulfilled\" ? x.value.output : String(x.reason));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789281614.743704,"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_zYALheM9uay70nIg1NjTUUog","id":"ctco_01a0997f-04e5-7f31-a876-99908bbcc85d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789281633.509858,"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.8 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"HTTP/2 307 \r\ncontent-type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\r\ncontent-length: 311\r\ndate: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 06:40:31 GMT\r\nlocation: /api/resolve-cache/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/2561c2c90560ae3df90ef8136f774faa5fb4b4e3/factorybench_lite%2Flevel_1.jsonl?%2Fdatasets%2FFactoryBench%2FFactoryBench%2Fresolve%2Fmain%2Ffactorybench_lite%2Flevel_1.jsonl=\u0026etag=%223e2fc640c33eb50f9cf7509d860ed2a3bb1dc745%22\r\nx-powered-by: huggingface-moon\r\nx-request-id: Root=1-6aa6455f-65dce76b53124b1232bb9f05\r\nratelimit: \"resolvers\";r=2999;t=29\r\nratelimit-policy: \"fixed window\";\"resolvers\";q=3000;w=300\r\ncross-origin-opener-policy: same-origin\r\nreferrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin\r\naccess-control-max-age: 86400\r\naccess-control-allow-origin: https://huggingface.co\r\nvary: Origin, Accept\r\naccess-control-expose-headers: X-Repo-Commit,X-Request-Id,X-Er