on Answering cite29†Time Series Forecasting L51: \\nL52: Modalities: cite30†Tabular cite31†Text L53: \\nL54: Formats: cite32†json L55: \\nL56: Languages: cite33†English L57: \\nL58: Size: cite34†< 1K L59: \\nL60: ArXiv:\\nL61: \\nL62: arxiv: 2202.03211\\nL63: \\nL64: arxiv: 2311.04153\\nL65: \\nL66: Tags: cite35†robotics cite36†industrial cite37†time-series cite38†causal-reasoning cite39†benchmark cite40†machine-understanding L67: \\nL68: Libraries: cite41†Datasets cite42†pandas cite43†Polars [Button: + 1]\\nL69: \\nL70: License:\\nL71: \\nL72: cc-by-4.0\\nL73: cite27†Dataset card cite44†Data Studio cite45†Files xet cite46†Community 1 L74: \\nL75: main\\nL76: \\nL77: cite45†FactoryBench / README.md\\nL78: \\nL79: cite47†Image: FactoryBench's picture L80: \\nL81: cite26†FactoryBench L82: \\nL83: Fix dataset card: point configs at the flat release layout\\nL84: \\nL85: cite48†2561c2c verified\\nL86: \\nL87: cite49†preview cite50†code L88: \\nL89: |\\nL90: \\nL91: cite51†Raw L92: \\nL93: Download with hf CLI\\nL94: \\nL95: Copy download link\\nL96: \\nL97: cite52†History cite53†Blame cite54†Contribute cite55†Delete L98: \\nL99: Safe\\nL100: \\nL101: 10.4 kB\\nL102: cite56†metadata L103: \\nL104:     license: cc-by-4.0\\nL105:     language:\\nL106:       - en\\nL107:     task_categories:\\nL108:       - question-answering\\nL109:       - time-series-forecasting\\nL110:     tags:\\nL111:       - robotics\\nL112:       - industrial\\nL113:       - time-series\\nL114:       - causal-reasoning\\nL115:       - benchmark\\nL116:       - machine-understanding\\nL117:     pretty_name: FactoryBench\\nL118:     size_categories:\\nL119:       - 10K<n<100K\\nL120:     configs:\\nL121:       - config_name: level_1\\nL122:         data_files:\\nL123:           - split: train\\nL124:             path: factorybench_qa/level_1.jsonl\\nL125:       - config_name: level_2\\nL126:         data_files:\\nL127:           - split: train\\nL128:             path: factorybench_qa/level_2.jsonl\\nL129:       - config_name: level_3\\nL130:         data_files:\\nL131:           - split: train\\nL132:             path: factorybench_qa/level_3.jsonl\\nL133:       - config_name: level_4\\nL134:         data_files:\\nL135:           - split: train\\nL136:             path: factorybench_qa/level_4.jsonl\\nL137:       - config_name: lite_level_1\\nL138:         data_files:\\nL139:           - split: train\\nL140:             path: factorybench_lite/level_1.jsonl\\nL141:       - config_name: lite_level_2\\nL142:         data_files:\\nL143:           - split: train\\nL144:             path: factorybench_lite/level_2.jsonl\\nL145:       - config_name: lite_level_3\\nL146:         data_files:\\nL147:           - split: train\\nL148:             path: factorybench_lite/level_3.jsonl\\nL149:       - config_name: lite_level_4\\nL150:         data_files:\\nL151:           - split: train\\nL152:             path: factorybench_lite/level_4.jsonl\\nL153: # FactoryBench\\nL154: \\nL155: FactoryBench is a benchmark for evaluating machine-behavior reasoning in time-series models and LLMs over industrial robotic telemetry. Question-answer pairs are organised along the four levels of Pearl's causal hierarchy:\\nL156: Level  | Capability  | Example\\nL157: --- | --- | ---\\nL158: L1 — State  | Identify the operational state from raw signals  | \\\"Which fault, if any, is occurring in this episode?\\\"\\nL159: L2 — Intervention  | Predict the effect of an intervention  | \\\"How would the joint torques change if the payload were doubled?\\\"\\nL160: L3 — Counterfactual  | Reason about alternative histories  | \\\"Would the collision still have occurred if the speed had been 50% lower?\\\"\\nL161: L4 — Decision  | Engineering decision-making (troubleshooting + optimisation)  | \\\"Given this anomaly, what is the most likely root cause and remediation?\\\"\\nL162: The benchmark is grounded in FactoryWave, a dense multivariate telemetry dataset collected from a UR3 collaborative robot (125 Hz) and a KUKA KR10 industrial arm (83 Hz), supplemented with the AURSAD and voraus-AD open-source datasets.\\nL163: ## Dataset summary\\nL164:   * 69,691 Q&A pairs across four causal levels, released as a single undivided pool (the public release is not split into train/validation/test).\\nL165:   * FactoryBench-Lite: a balanced 3,000-item subset for cheap evaluation, even across templates and within each template on the dimension that determines its answer.\\nL166:   * 5 answer formats: single-select MCQ, multi-select MCQ, ranking, tensor/numerical, free-form (judged by an LLM-as-judge voting protocol).\\nL167:   * Telemetry from real industrial robots with systematic fault injection (27 atomic mechanisms across pick-and-place, screwing, and peg-in-hole tasks).\\nL168: ## Repository layout\\nL169: \\nL170:     `FactoryBench/\\nL171:     ├── factorybench_qa/                 # Question-answer pairs (full pool)\\nL172:     │   ├── level_1.jsonl\\nL173:     │   ├── level_2.jsonl\\nL174:     │   ├── level_3.jsonl\\nL175:     │   └── level_4.jsonl\\nL176:     ├── factorybench_lite/               # Balanced 3,000-item evaluation subset\\nL177:     │   ├── level_1.jsonl\\nL178:     │   ├── level_2.jsonl\\nL179:     │   ├── level_3.jsonl\\nL180:     │   └── level_4.jsonl\\nL181:     ├── knowledge_graph/                 # Combined knowledge graph\\nL182:     │   ├── knowledge_graph.json         # Machines, grippers, tasks, events, faults, anomalies, error→protocol map, relevance specs\\nL183:     │   └── SCHEMA.md                    # Field-level schema documentation\\nL184:     └── factorywave/                     # Underlying telemetry & metadata\\nL185:         ├── episodes.parquet             # Episode-level metadata (9,728 episodes)\\nL186:         ├── flow.parquet                 # Task flow definitions\\nL187:         ├── kuka_signals.parquet         # KUKA KR10 signals (~83 Hz, 1,428 episodes)\\nL188:         ├── ur_signals.parquet           # UR3 signals (~125 Hz, 3,076 episodes)\\nL189:         ├── ur_signals_10hz.parquet      # UR3 signals (10 Hz, 3,984 episodes — disjoint from ur_signals)\\nL190:         └── ur_screwdriver_signals.parquet  # UR3 screwdriver subset (~125 Hz, 1,240 episodes)\\nL191:     `\\nL192: > Note on UR coverage. `ur_signals.parquet` (~125 Hz) and `ur_signals_10hz.parquet` (10 Hz) cover disjoint UR3 episode subsets — no episode appears in both files. Together they span 7,060 distinct UR3 episodes; `ur_screwdriver_signals.parquet` adds another 1,240 screwdriver-task episodes. Each row in `episodes.parquet` corresponds to exactly one signal table.\\nL193: ## Knowledge graph\\nL194: `knowledge_graph/knowledge_graph.json` is the structured \\\"world model\\\" that grounds FactoryBench Q&A items: machine and gripper capability tables, the task/event vocabulary, the fault root-cause catalogue, the observable-anomaly catalogue, the error→protocol mapping used to derive ground-truth answers for L4 troubleshooting items, an anomaly severity ranking, and the relevance specs that drive fault-aware sub-series sampling. See cite57†`knowledge_graph/SCHEMA.md` for field-level documentation.\\nL195:     `import json, urllib.request\\nL196:     url = \\\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/resolve/main/knowledge_graph/knowledge_graph.json\\\"\\nL197:     kg = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(url).read())\\nL198: \\nL199:     # Machine spec lookup\\nL200:     machines_by_id = {m[\\\"machine_id\\\"]: m for m in kg[\\\"machines\\\"]}\\nL201: \\nL202:     # Error → operator protocol (the ground truth for L4 troubleshooting answers)\\nL203:     protocol_for = {e[\\\"root_cause\\\"]: e[\\\"ur3_protocol\\\"] for e in kg[\\\"root_cause_error_mapping\\\"]}\\nL204:     print(protocol_for[\\\"collision_rigid_object\\\"])\\nL205:     `\\nL206: ## Q&A pair counts\\nL207: \\nL208: Level  | Full pool  | Lite\\nL209: --- | --- | ---\\nL210: L1  | 15,321  | 572\\nL211: L2  | 40,226  | 1,430\\nL212: L3  | 2,939  | 712\\nL213: L4  | 11,205  | 286\\nL214: Total  | 69,691  | 3,000\\nL215: \\nL216: The public release is a single undivided pool per level. The `train` split name in the config block is the Hugging Face default for a single-file config, not a semantic split. A private 15% slice is held back for contamination checks and is not counted here.\\nL217: ## Q&A fields\\nL218: \\nL219: Each line in `factorybench_qa/level_*.jsonl` (and `factorybench_lite/level_*.jsonl`) is a single Q&A item:\\nL220: Field  | Description\\nL221: --- | ---\\nL222: `id`  | Unique item identifier\\nL223: `level`  | Causal level (1–4)\\nL224: `template_id`  | Question template the item was generated from\\nL225: `template_type`  | Answer format (`single_choice`, `multi_choice`, `ranking`, `tensor`, `free_form`)\\nL226: `hides`  | Channels/fields hidden from the model in this item\\nL227: `question`  | Natural-language question\\nL228: `options`  | Answer options (for MCQ/ranking templates)\\nL229: `answer`  | Ground-truth answer\\nL230: `root_cause`  | Underlying fault/cause (Level 4 only)\\nL231: `acceptance_bounds`  | Tolerance for numerical answers\\nL232: `provenance`  | Source episode(s) and channels used to derive the item\\nL233: `context`  | Time-series and metadata context exposed to the model\\nL234: ## Loading the data\\nL235: \\nL236:     `from datasets import load_dataset\\nL237: \\nL238:     # Load one level of the full pool\\nL239:     ds = load_dataset(\\\"FactoryBench/FactoryBench\\\", \\\"level_1\\\", split=\\\"train\\\")\\nL240: \\nL241:     # Load one level of the balanced Lite subset\\nL242:     lite = load_dataset(\\\"FactoryBench/FactoryBench\\\", \\\"lite_level_1\\\", split=\\\"train\\\")\\nL243: \\nL244:     # Or address a file directly\\nL245:     ds = load_dataset(\\nL246:         \\\"FactoryBench/FactoryBench\\\",\\nL247:         data_files=\\\"factorybench_qa/level_1.jsonl\\\",\\nL248:         split=\\\"train\\\",\\nL249:     )\\nL250:     # Load underlying telemetry\\nL251:     import pandas as pd\\nL252:     root = \\\"hf://datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/factorywave\\\"\\nL253:     episodes = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/episodes.parquet\\\")\\nL254:     ur_125 = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/ur_signals.parquet\\\")\\nL255:     ur_10  = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/ur_signals_10hz.parquet\\\")  # different episodes\\nL256:     kuka   = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/kuka_signals.parquet\\\")\\nL257:     screw  = pd.read_parquet(f\\\"{root}/ur_screwdriver_signals.parquet\\\")\\nL258:     # Load the combined knowledge graph (machines, faults, error→protocol, ...)\\nL259:     import json, urllib.request\\nL260:     kg = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(\\nL261:         \\\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/FactoryBench/FactoryBench/resolve/main/knowledge_graph/knowledge_graph.json\\\"\\nL262:     ).read())\\nL263:     `\\nL264: ## Citation\\nL265: If you use FactoryBench, please cite the dataset and the two upstream open-source datasets it incorporates (AURSAD and voraus-AD):\\nL266: \\nL267:     `@misc{anonymous2026factorybench,\\nL268:       title  = {FactoryBench: Evaluating Industrial Machine Understanding},\\nL269:       author = {Anonymous},\\nL270:       year   = {2026},\\nL271:       note   = {Submission under double-blind review}\\nL272:     }\\nL273: \\nL274:     @article{leporowski2022aursad,\\nL275:       title   = {{AURSAD}: Universal Robot Screwdriving Anomaly Detection Dataset},\\nL276:       author  = {Leporowski, B{\\\\l}a{\\\\.z}ej and Tola, Daniella and Hansen, Christian and Iosifidis, Alexandros},\\nL277:       journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.03211},\\nL278:       year    = {2022}\\nL279:     }\\nL280: \\nL281:     @misc{brockmann2024vorausad,\\nL282:       title         = {voraus-{AD}: A New Dataset for Anomaly Detection in Robot Applications},\\nL283:       author        = {Brockmann, Jan Thie{\\\\ss} and Rudolph, Marco and Rosenhahn, Bodo and Wandt, Bastian},\\nL284:       year          = {2024},\\nL285:       eprint        = {2311.04153},\\nL286:       archivePrefix = {arXiv},\\nL287:       primaryClass  = {cs.RO}\\nL288:     }\\nL289:     `\\nL290: ## Intended use & limitations\\nL291: \\nL292: Intended use. Benchmark evaluation of LLMs and time-series models on structured industrial Q&A reasoning tasks (state, intervention, counterfactual, decision-making).\\nL293: Limitations. Domain-specific to factory and industrial robotic scenarios; may not generalise to open-domain Q&A. Faults are atomic and drawn from a closed catalogue of 27 physically injected mechanisms — different from compound or gradual real-world faults. The dataset shows a size imbalance between Levels 2 and 3.\\nL294: \\nL295: Out-of-scope. Not intended for deployment in safety-critical, medical, legal, or financial decision systems without further validation by domain experts.\\nL296: ## License\\nL297: \\nL298: Released under the cite58†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)†creativecommons.org license. You may share and adapt the dataset for any purpose, including commercial, with appropriate attribution to the FactoryBench authors. The accompanying generator source code, evaluation scripts, LLM-as-judge prompts, and tooling (linked from the paper) are released separately under the MIT License.\\n\""}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0997d-e59a-7ed3-9008-5472efcdac29","create_time":1789281793.703155}}}
